  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "TRIAL EDITION - SQL longer than 10,000 characters is rejected (parse() returns -1). Full edition: https://www.sqlparser.com\n\nRelease 4.1.0.11\n\n- [DB2] Accept a comma-separated condition-value list after\n  DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR, mixing the specific forms (SQLSTATE,\n  condition name) with the general ones (SQLEXCEPTION, SQLWARNING,\n  NOT FOUND), for example\n  DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION, SQLWARNING. Previously only\n  the specific forms could be listed. Applies to CONTINUE, EXIT and\n  UNDO handlers alike.\n- [DB2] Accept RESIGNAL with no condition, which re-raises the\n  condition currently being handled and is the usual form inside a\n  handler. A bare SIGNAL is still rejected, since DB2 requires a\n  condition there.\n  Known limitation: a SQLSTATE given as a variable rather than a\n  string literal (SIGNAL SQLSTATE VALUE my_var) and a module-qualified\n  condition name (schema.condition) are still unsupported.\n\nDemos: https://github.com/sqlparser/gsp_demo_dotnet",
